    "answer_en": "1 Check all the wires are connected as they should be. And please measure the voltage of the +BAT- terminals (11# and 12#) on the control board to see whether it is above 22V.\n2 If the voltage is normal, please check whether the power led on the control board is ON. If the power led is off, check the fuse whether it was burnt out and replace the fuse if necessary. There is a backup fuse packed within the user manual pack. The fuse type is ∅5*20mm 10A 250VAC fast blow glass fuse.\n3 If the voltage is below 22V, disconnect the solar charge controller from the solar panels, and disconnect the solar controller from the control board. Use a multi-meter to test the voltage of the batteries, as well as the output voltage from the LOAD terminals of the solar charger controller.\na. If battery voltage is above 22 VDC, but there's no output voltage from LOAD of the solar charger controller or the output voltage is too low, then the solar charger controller is defective.\nb. If the battery voltage is less than 22 VDC, please fully charge the batteries with an additional charger and do the test again.\nc. If battery voltage is always below 22VDC even if fully charged, replace the batteries, fully charge new batteries and try again.",
